 ],
 "literal": "Even if love ceases, / as the great rain fell with its assemblage at midnight, / the waterfall resounding inside the mountain clefts, O lord of such a land! / Will our relationship too fade on your side?",
 "translation": "Even if passion fades, O lord of the land where waterfalls resound inside mountain clefts as great rain-clouds pour down in the middle of the night, will our relationship too fade away from your heart?",
 "modern": "நள்ளிரவில் திரண்ட கரிய மேகங்கள் மழையாகப் பொழிந்ததால், மலைப்பிளவுகளின் உள்ளே அருவிகள் ஆரவாரிக்கும் நாட்டின் தலைவனே! நம்மிடையே உள்ள காதல் காமம் ஒருவேளை நீங்கிப்போனாலும், உன்னிடத்தில் எங்களோடு உள்ள உறவும் தேய்ந்துபோய்விடுமோ?",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine's confidante (tōḻi) to the hero (thalaivan) during his secret night visits. She describes his mountainous land (kuriñci tiṇai) where heavy rains fall at midnight, causing waterfalls to roar loudly inside hidden mountain clefts. This imagery of hidden waterfalls making a loud noise represents their secret love affair, which is now becoming known to others and causing gossip. By asking if their relationship (toṭarpu) will fade once the initial physical passion (kāmam) of these secret meetings decreases, she subtly urges him to stop the secret visits and marry the heroine formally. The key contrast is between 'kāmam' (temporary physical desire) and 'toṭarpu' (enduring relationship).",
